{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793463497,"nonce":"h0O9sWbsct19/HSi7NEd+x88WAM=","title":"LLVM tier: unpredictable branches turned into selects make collatz and branchy slower than Cranelift","message":"After dd108e8b, the rows the LLVM tier still slows (Mac, same binary, wall ms, LLVM on / Cranelift alone): collatz 242.7 / 170.5, branchy 99.0 / 77.4, records 62.1 / 57.6, tokenize 121.5 / 114.7, closure_call 20.3 / 18.9. The tier is worth it on op_overload (53.4 / 116.6), method_call, nbody and pipeline.\n\ncollatz: the LLVM install of main takes about 80 ms (opt 38, codegen 30) and the frame transfers into it mid-loop; the code it runs is slower in steady state (900000-element variant: 713 to 764 ms with the tier, 390 to 520 without). Its post-optimisation IR turns `if x % 2 == 0 then x = x / 2 else x = 3 * x + 1 end` (x a float after the first halving) into both arms and a select fed by the floor-based modulo, so each iteration waits on fmul, floor, fmul, fsub, fcmp and select before the next; Cranelift keeps the branch and the CPU speculates past it.\n\n`ZYNTAX_LLVM_ARGS=-two-entry-phi-node-folding-threshold=0` (SimplifyCFG speculates nothing to fold a two-entry phi) puts collatz at 142.9 ms, under Cranelift and LuaJIT (176), but costs mandelbrot 39.3 -\u003e 49.8, nbody 128.1 -\u003e 136.1, records 62.1 -\u003e 66.7 and tokenize 121.5 -\u003e 128.7; -phi-node-folding-threshold=0 does not help collatz. branchy moves with neither, so its loss has another cause (the seven-way chain on acc % 7, 50fc717).\n\nDirections: choose select or branch per loop from what the tier-0 code saw (branch weights from the baseline's counters would let LLVM's SelectOptimize keep a 50/50 branch), or keep a loop at Cranelift when its LLVM code would not be faster.","files":null}]}